s so  archived newspaper resources from the  new york public library for example were  incredibly valuable um ancestry.com and  how they've digitized um genealogy  records and the census is incredible  like that made so much of this book  possible so like the quakers figuring  out what streets they lived on who was  near who um where lizzy was living at  different points in her life um that was  really really critical but there was a  lot of this book that wasn't digital i  think that there's i think his mindset's  changing a little bit but um i think  among a lot of journalists and a lot of  readers there's this idea that if it's  not on google it's like not information  like it's not real  and i kept finding over and over again  um  these gems that were just like in  people's attics or  um tucked away and thing i mean just so  much of this book was not out there  digitally um james mcgee her father um  this didn't even end up in the book but  his civil war letters were at the new  york historical society and so you get  the gloves and you paw through them and  you start to get a portrait of who this  guy really was and that so things like  that made these people into real human  beings as opposed to  textbook names so  it was a totally different process in  some ways from doing a newspaper story  because  most of the people you're in this case i  was writing about were deceased um ralph  was a huge resource and i've never  asked more of somebody in terms of time  in terms of walking me through  the timeline of how he found things he  had a lot of notes so he had documented  a lot of this really really well i  interviewed his sons i interviewed his  um his ex-wife actually passed away  there were three people um who who died  in the time i was reporting this so i  mean i i was like gathering as much as i  could um both interviews but then a lot  of things it was so frustrating like i  had so many moments where i thought like  barton why can't i just ask you a  question or daryl like what did you  think about this so  there was a lot of genealogy um  and uh but it was a hodgepodge of  everything and it was actually  overwhelming that was like the hardest  part was gathering all the string and  then actually putting it together it  felt very very unwieldy a lot of the  time  uh yes  so ralph must be thrilled  and i was wondering  um if there were things that you found  that he had never known  can you talk a little bit about that  yeah so ralph emailed me just a couple  days ago and his son  is actually a lawyer in new york and  wrote his harvard law admissions essay  about the anti-monopoly trial and how  growing up in courtrooms made him want  to be he's a labor lawyer um so i've  been in touch with them and ralph  emailed me  i was asking how he was doing and he  splits his time now between the states  and europe which is not a bad way to  live and he said life begins at 89 and i  just thought that was like so amazing i  was like gosh we should all be that  lucky to be so  spry in our 80s um but i did so the  lizzie mcgee pieces he found out he is  by far the expert on a lot of this and  he in the course of his case and he was  an economist before this i mean we were  dealing with somebody who had a very  sophisticated knowledge of henry george  and tax theory and kind of the context  of how it fit together um and he became  like a paralegal in a lot of ways  working on this case too  um but the lizzie mcgee pieces he knew  she existed but kind of the deeper stuff  and her biography was something i spent  a lot of time on  um and there were things that he just  wouldn't have needed to be interested in  so like the quakers and the history of  atlantic city that's in the book and  kind of what it was like for them living  there that was something i was much more  interested in  as a narrative storyteller  because he was living in berkeley in the  70s  it makes a lot of sense that he wouldn't  have had to zoom out and look at  