ike i was reporting a long journal or  time story which means no surprises um  and you want as many people to  participate as you can so i had  contacted hasbro for comment several  times in the process and there was some  turnover and to be honest i'm not sure i  didn't follow like the play-by-play of  who was heading what part of pr then um  but i sent them hundreds of  fact-checking questions that weren't  answered um for the times excerpt of the  book that ran i contacted them they  didn't respond so um you know it's a  bummer when somebody doesn't want to  participate but um because they acquired  parker brothers in 1991 um i mean most  of the book takes place before then and  a lot of the documents um i was very  fortunate because i tried to give  everybody a voice in the story and  luckily barton had a deposition so i  feel like we did get to hear from him um  we have the daryl letters we have things  that um quite frankly i don't know what  documents they would have had because  they acquired the company so late so  even though they didn't participate  um researching the history of parker  brothers and the people was really  really important to me and a lot of that  because they were just such a noted  company was out there it took some  digging but but there was there was some  stuff out there  i don't believe so um but it's funny  because in the depositions there and you  can tell it's like a lot of lawyers like  there's that's a common typo that people  make and it really like i went through  the book like the night before it closed  and i was like i have to make sure  there's no clarence darrow's in here  like this is a book about charles darrow  so  i don't believe there's a relation but  who knows  um it's a relatively common  last name i believe  yes  and now you've seen a lot of how kind of  the history has been changed in terms of  how who looks to the creator of monopoly  what's really being done to honor the  legacy of mcgee  that's a wonderful question  um the short answer is i don't know um  she didn't have any children and um i  did my best to contact distant relatives  but by then they're so distant that you  know  it is what it is  um there isn't really anything tangible  like in place so after ralph's trial he  went about really trying to make sure  the quakers of atlantic city were not  acknowledged and he put up this plaque  and so  i guess i mean my version of it is just  telling her story  i mean it seems like most i can't even  tell you how many times the last few  years people go oh you're writing a book  a monopoly right the depression game and  i just i'm like uh you know it's like  it's so i i think that part one is just  getting the story out there but i don't  know of anything tangible if you have  ideas i'd be all ears  yes  i remember the anti-monopoly game in  sports back in the 70s we  that's a great question every now and  then i and i'm not like a game collector  or like a hardcore like appraiser every  now and then one pops up on ebay um  they're not if they're expensive they're  not like ludicrously so or at least i  haven't seen them um there was quite a  lot there was it was really successful  when it started so there were a lot of  them in circulation um so as far as i  know they're not really really expensive  um and in general board games are this  weird collectible because it's kind of  similar to what i think comic books went  through a generation ago where people  just thought they weren't valuable and  they were just you know people were  stuffing like plumbing leaks with them  and i mean which if you're a superman  fan just like you know makes you like  choke up um but uh i think board games  are cultural artifacts and i think they  are valuable and they do reflect times  that they were made in so i hope more  anti-monopoly games get preserved um and  he's changed it a little bit so so  they're out there but i almost wish  people valued them more because i think  it's a to 